{:bash:}[bash]
{:zsh:}[zsh]
{:prompt:}[prompt]
{:vim-mode:}[vim mode]

* Quote values and pass them as arguments
@code bash
file='report with spaces.txt'
printf '%s\n' "$file"
# -- ends option parsing, even if the filename starts with -
cat -- "$file"
@end
Single quotes preserve literal text; double quotes expand variables while keeping each value together.

* Check a command's exit status
@code bash
if rg -n -- 'needle' file.txt; then
    printf '%s\n' 'Found a match'
else
    status=$?
    printf 'Search exited with status %s\n' "$status" >&2
fi
@end
Capture `$?` immediately. For ripgrep, 1 means no match; 2 means an error.

* Detect failures in a pipeline (Bash)
@code bash
set -o pipefail
if cat -- input.txt | sort > sorted.txt; then
    printf '%s\n' 'Sorted successfully'
else
    printf '%s\n' 'Reading or sorting failed' >&2
fi
@end
Without `pipefail`, only the last command determines a pipeline's status. This does not prevent a partial output file on failure.

* Loop over arbitrary filenames (Bash)
@code bash
while IFS= read -r -d '' file; do
    printf '%s\n' "$file"
done < <(find . -type f -print0)
@end
NUL separators preserve spaces, wildcard characters, and newlines in filenames. This uses Bash process substitution.

Related: {:array:}[Bash arrays], {:jq:}[jq]. Reference: {https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/bash.html}[Bash manual].
